# When to Convert a Dashboard to a Tableau Data App

Dashboards built by {{ dashboard_app }} are vibe-coded HTML/CSS/JS apps that
get their data from MCP tool calls. That is the right shape for exploration
and personal or small-team use. At some point a dashboard outgrows it and
should be converted to a Tableau data app: the same HTML/CSS/JS, looking
identical, embedded in a published workbook, with Tableau querying the data
instead of MCP.

There is no strict rule for when that point arrives. Use the signals below as
judgment aids, not a checklist, and explain your reasoning with evidence.

## What conversion gains

Conversion moves the dashboard onto Tableau's scale and governance:

- **Scale:** Tableau runs the queries, so larger result sets, complex data
  models, and many concurrent viewers no longer depend on MCP tool calls.
- **Sharing:** the dashboard is published content with a URL, project
  placement, and Tableau permissions instead of a file or chat artifact.
- **Governance:** a published datasource brings user access permissions,
  row-level security, datasource certification, and lineage.

Every completed conversion uses the **published-datasource path**. Reuse a
credible published datasource when one exists; otherwise create and publish a
TDS or TDSX first. A TDSX backed by a materialized extract centralizes access
and removes browser-side file loading, but it is still a snapshot unless a
supported refresh path is configured. Never present a static published extract
as live or automatically refreshed.

## Classify the source first

Classify the dashboard's source exactly as
[step 2 of the conversion](../SKILL.md#2-inspect-the-data-dashboard-and-provenance)
does, and reuse that classification if it was already done:

1. **Flat files:** the data came from CSV, TSV, Excel, or a local
   extract/export.
2. **Connector-backed MCP:** the data came from an MCP tool call backed by a
   system Tableau has an equivalent connector for.
3. **Neither:** an MCP tool call backed by a system no Tableau connector
   reaches.

Then check whether a credible published datasource for the same data already
exists. If you have not searched yet, say that the data path depends on it
rather than assuming either outcome.

The class sets which volume threshold applies and what conversion can provide:

| Source class | Volume threshold | Published datasource | Data path | What conversion provides | How to recommend |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Flat files | Over 1M rows or 5M cells (rows × columns) loaded | Credible match exists | Published datasource | Scale, sharing, full governance | Weigh the signals normally |
| Flat files | Over 1M rows or 5M cells loaded | None | Create and publish TDSX, then wire it | Scale, sharing, centralized permissions; freshness depends on refresh setup | Recommend conversion and disclose refresh requirements |
| Connector-backed MCP | 1,000+ marks in a single visual | Credible match exists | Published datasource | Scale, sharing, full governance | Weigh the signals normally |
| Connector-backed MCP | 1,000+ marks in a single visual | None | Create live TDS when connection/auth are available; otherwise publish TDSX extract | Scale and sharing; full freshness/governance only with the live or refreshed path | Recommend conversion and name the feasible publish path |
| Neither | 1,000+ marks in a single visual | Not possible | Publish materialized results as TDSX extract | Scale and centralized access to the snapshot, not live freshness | Recommend only when sharing/centralization justifies a snapshot and disclose refresh limits |

## Strong signals

Any one of these is usually enough to recommend conversion.

**Data volume:** measured on the data the dashboard works with, not on the
underlying database tables. Use the threshold for the source class in the
table above; each class has one threshold, not both.

- **Marks** are the bars, points, lines, cells, or other elements drawn from
  the data that one chart or table needs. Count them per visual, not across
  the whole dashboard.
- **Rows and cells** are counted on the flat-file data the dashboard loads,
  including any inline data.
- In any class, a tool call's results were truncated, paginated to work
  around a limit, or sampled to fit.

**Data modeling:** measured on what the dashboard combines itself, across tool
calls or in its own code.

- Data from more than one source system is combined, for example Snowflake
  plus SQL Server.
- Two or more fact tables at different grains are joined (multi-fact).

**Connectivity:**

- The source requires authentication that is tied to the author's MCP session.
  Other viewers would need their own credentials, or the dashboard would expose
  data under the author's identity.

**Publishing:**

- The dashboard is shared with, or intended for, more than 3 people.
- It is still being used or shared 90 days after creation.
- Viewers should see only the rows or content they are entitled to (row-level
  security or per-user permissions).

## Supporting signals

Two or more of these together usually justify recommending conversion. One
alone is a reason to mention it, not to push.

- More than 2 joins are performed across tool calls or in app code.
- Object-model relationships (logical tables joined at query time by
  relationship rules) are reimplemented in app code.
- Business metrics, calculations, or groupings are defined in app code rather
  than in a governed datasource.
- The numbers are quoted in decisions, decks, or recurring reviews, so they
  need a certified source and lineage.
- The data is sensitive (PII, financial, or regulated) and the audience is
  growing.
- The dashboard needs refreshed data on a schedule rather than whenever
  someone reruns the tool calls.

## Reasons to keep it as is

Recommend staying put, or say the case is weak, when:

- it is one-off exploration or a personal working view;
- each visual draws few marks, the data comes from a single source, and the
  app does no modeling of its own;
- the audience is the author or a few people who already have access to the
  data;
- the source class is "Neither" and the user has no sharing need (see the
  table above).

## Gathering evidence

Check what you can from the dashboard artifact itself before asking:

- the number of MCP tool calls and their sources, enough to classify the
  source;
- the volume measure for that class: marks per visual, from the data bound to
  each chart or table, or rows and columns of the loaded flat-file data;
- joins, merges, and lookups across result sets in the JavaScript;
- calculations and groupings defined in app code;
- whether a published datasource for the same source already exists, and
  whether it is certified.

Some facts can only come from the user: audience size, how long the dashboard
has been in use, whether it is shared, how sensitive the data is, and whether
row-level restrictions apply. Ask for these in one batched question, and only
for the facts that would change the recommendation.

## Presenting the recommendation

- Lead with the recommendation: convert, consider converting, or keep as is.
- List the signals that apply, each with its evidence: what the code shows or
  what the user said.
- Name the source class and the data path the conversion would use, and say
  what that path does and does not provide. When no credible datasource exists,
  say that the conversion will create and publish a TDS/TDSX before publishing
  the app.
- Never start a conversion based on these signals alone. Recommend it, and
  wait for the user to agree.
